<h2>An ex parte decree meets a wakf</h2>
<p>The first respondent sued for specific performance of a 2001 agreement to sell shop property at Rikabgunj, Pathergatti, Hyderabad. The defendant never appeared, and in March 2015 the suit was decreed ex parte — sale deed, possession and costs. When execution was taken out in 2016 for physical possession, Alladin Charities and Zakat Wakf stepped in as a third-party objector, tracing its title to a Farman of the Nizam, a Muntakhab of 1949, a 1976 trust deed and proceedings under the Waqf Act, 1995 culminating in a 2003 gazette notification.</p>
<p>The executing court dismissed the objection in 2019 — holding, among other things, that an objection framed under Order XXI Rule 58 did not lie against execution for possession and that the wakf had not proved the property was notified wakf property. The High Court affirmed, adding doubts about the trustee&rsquo;s authority and gaps in linking municipal numbers to the gazetted shops. The Supreme Court has now unwound both orders.</p>

<h2>The statutory scheme the Code prescribes</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Rule 97.</strong> The decree-holder who meets resistance or obstruction applies to the executing court, which must adjudicate the obstructionist&rsquo;s claim.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Rule 101.</strong> All questions of right, title and interest between the parties are determined by the executing court itself — a separate suit is neither necessary nor permitted.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Rule 98 read with Rule 100.</strong> The court then orders possession, or upholds the objector, as the adjudication warrants; the order has the force of a decree.</div>
</div>
<p>Relying on <em>Periyammal v. V. Rajamani</em> (2025), the Court emphasised that this adjudication must precede delivery of the property. A decree-holder cannot leapfrog a pending objection by seeking a fresh warrant of possession under Rule 35.</p>

<h2>Why the remand matters</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Strangers get a real trial</h4><p>An objector claiming independent title is entitled to a full inquiry — pleadings, evidence, findings — not a summary brush-off on technical labels.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Labels do not defeat substance</h4><p>That an objection cited Rule 58 rather than Rule 97 did not justify refusing the inquiry the Code mandates for possession executions.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Identity of property is central</h4><p>Where title turns on old municipal and revenue records, the executing court must permit the parties to prove the linkage rather than resolving doubts against the objector at the threshold.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Takeaways for execution practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For decree-holders:</strong> an ex parte decree is only the beginning where third parties hold possession; budget for a contested Rule 97 inquiry and marshal title and identity evidence early.</li>
    <li><strong>For objectors:</strong> file promptly, plead the independent right with documents, and seek adjudication under Rules 97 and 101 — the executing court is the forum, and its refusal to inquire is itself appealable.</li>
    <li><strong>For both:</strong> the adjudication under Rule 98 or Rule 100 carries the force of a decree, so the inquiry is effectively a trial — treat the evidence accordingly.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Execution proceedings turn on their own records, and the judgment should be read in full before being relied upon.</p></div>
